    {
      "content": "#include \"knn.h\"\n\nchar knn(int n_groups, Group * groups, int k, Point to_evaluate) {\n    char * labels = (char *) malloc(sizeof(char) * k);\n    float * distances = (float *) malloc(sizeof(float) * k);\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {\n        labels[i] = -1;\n        distances[i] = -1.0f;\n    }\n\n    #pragma omp parallel for collapse(2) schedule(dynamic)\n    for (int i = 0; i < n_groups; i++) {\n        for (int j = 0; j < groups[i].length; j++) {\n            float d = euclidean_distance_no_sqrt(to_evaluate, groups[i].points[j]);\n            #pragma omp critical\n            {\n                int x;\n                for (x = 0; x < k; x++) {\n                    if (d < distances[x] || distances[x] == -1.0f) {\n                        for (int y = k - 1; y > x; y--) {\n                            distances[y] = distances[y - 1];\n                            labels[y] = labels[y - 1];\n                        }\n                        distances[x] = d;\n                        labels[x] = groups[i].label;\n                        break;\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    qsort(labels, k, sizeof(char), compare_for_sort);\n\n    char most_frequent = labels[0];\n    int most_frequent_count = 1;\n    int current_frequency = 1;\n\n    for (int i = 1; i < k; i++) {\n        if (labels[i] != labels[i - 1]) {\n            if (current_frequency > most_frequent_count) {\n                most_frequent = labels[i - 1];\n                most_frequent_count = current_frequency;\n            }\n            current_frequency = 1;\n        } else {\n            current_frequency++;\n        }\n\n        if (i == k - 1 && current_frequency > most_frequent_count) {\n            most_frequent = labels[i - 1];\n            most_frequent_count = current_frequency;\n        }\n    }\n\n    free(labels);\n    free(distances);\n\n    return most_frequent;\n}\n",
      "path": "src/knn.c"
    }
  ],
  "hypothesis": "Using OpenMP collapse(2) with dynamic scheduling on the flattened group-point iteration space distributes individual point evaluations evenly across threads, while a critical section protects global top-k updates from race conditions, eliminating per-thread local arrays and merge overhead.",
  "notes": "This approach avoids the overhead of merging per-thread results by directly updating a shared global top-k array inside a critical section. Since k is small, contention on the critical section is expected to be low.",
  "sources_kept": [],
